{
  "verdict": "faithful_with_caveats",
  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Relative to P2's informal mention of \"recurrent\" and \"feedforward systems\", `S1 Bool Bool` idealizes systems as deterministic, discrete-time, synchronous, time-invariant (non-plastic) systems with finitely many units (`Fintype A1`) and Boolean inputs/outputs. P2 does not explicitly require finiteness of units or time-invariance, although finiteness of units is necessary for the counterexample and matches \"static FNNs regardless of their size\" in [P-O2]."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Feedforwardness (`N.D9`) is defined via acyclicity of the one-step functional dependency graph (`N.D8`) quantified over all global states (including unreachable ones), while `N : S1 Bool Bool` allows arbitrary (possibly infinite) per-node state types and arbitrary update and readout functions. Evaluating dependency over unreachable states slightly restricts what counts as feedforward compared to a reachable-state notion (though it is broader than fixed structural wiring), whereas allowing arbitrary state types and functions strengthens the non-existence claim relative to standard weighted FNNs."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Relative to [P-O2], `Target` does not formalize general RNNs, weighted neural architectures, or persistent perturbations in hidden states, nor does it address approximate replication; instead, it instantiates the temporal-persistence impossibility via a single concrete one-unit Boolean latch (`d15`) and exact input-output equivalence over all finite Boolean sequences (`S1.D6`)."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Target faithfully formalizes the claimed unbounded-horizon counterexample to P2—a single static, recurrent one-unit Boolean system whose input-output map over all finite sequences cannot be matched by any finite-unit acyclic synchronous system—and bears appropriately on [P-O2]'s claim about static FNNs. The only divergences are minor modeling idealizations (deterministic, discrete-time, finite-unit, all-states functional acyclicity) and the restriction to exact input-output non-replicability of a single Boolean latch rather than general weighted RNNs."
}
